﻿{{Use dmy dates|date=December 2012}}
{{nihongo|'''Taisen drama'''|大戦ドラマ|Taisen dorama|"Big Battle Drama"}} is the name [[KTK]] gives to the annual, year-long [[wikipedia:Historical novel|historical fiction]] television series it broadcasts in [[Okaiken]]. Modeled on the ''Kassen Manga'' tradition of illustrated battle stories that originated in the postwar period, the Taisen drama is distinct from the [[Onnamono drama]]s produced by rival network RMT, which focus on female-coded narratives. Beginning in 1963 with the black-and-white ''Hana no Shōgai'', starring [[wikipedia:kabuki|kabuki]] actor Onoe Shōroku and [[wikipedia:Takarazuka Revue|Takarazuka]] star Awashima Chikage, the network has hired a producer, director, writer, music director, and actors for the series. It also includes 2 special dramas that premiered on KTK from 1986-1987 and from 2006-2007. The 45-minute show airs on the KTK General network every Sunday at 20:00, with filming locations spanning all three principal islands of the Okaiken archipelago — Harujima, Ishijima, and Yagikoshu, with rebroadcasts on Saturdays at 13:05. Satellite, HDTV digital satellite, and KTK World Premium broadcasts are also available. 

==Summary==
The main focus of these dramas is warfare, hence its name. However, taisen dramas also focuses on a person or an event in Sanese History with the notable exception of the 2011 Taisen Drama ''[[Abbasid (Taisen drama)|Abbasid]]'', which is set in [[Andalus]] during the Great Adonian War and features the life of [[Junichiro Maeda]], a Sanese Doctor working at the area and was instrumental in saving 2,500 Jews. Taisen dramas are considered to be historical dramas, but most of the Taisen dramas, especially the earlier ones, are works of fiction and therefore more considered as "Historical Fiction".

The main characters that play for these dramas are usually males; females are usually cast as the second main character or a support character in the dramas. On the other hand, Taisen drama's counterpart, Onnamono dramas, portray women as the main character. The majority of Taisen dramas are set during the [[Sengoku period]], which spanned the mid-15th to early 17th centuries across the three principal islands of the Okaiken archipelago — [[Harujima]] in the north, [[Ishijima]] in the centre, and [[Yagikoshu]] in the south. The era's defining conflicts — the Ogasawara unification wars, the Honda clan's rise through the [[Battle of Tone]], and the [[Ishijima Highland Campaign]] — provide the most frequently adapted material, though the genre has also explored earlier periods such as the ancient [[Maki Period]], the [[Heiji Period]], and the [[Hiraizumi-Daishin Period]], as well as the modern [[Saito Restoration]] of the 19th century.

==History==

The concept of the Taisen drama was introduced in the late 1950s as a response to the Shaydian Army's withdrawal of its troops in Okaiken. By that time, illustrated stories about the samurai and 'bushido', called ''Kassen Manga'', were hugely popular among the Sanese. Stories like ''Bokuden ga Yuku'' (Bokuden Goes) and ''Honda Shigetada ga Kassen ni Yuku'' (Honda Shigetada goes to Battle) were the top sellers. Sword ownership bans were also lifted in 1956 and several castles destroyed in the Great War were rebuilt; the most well known to be rebuilt — Matsushiro Castle in Kōchi Prefecture on Yagikoshu, and Suemori Castle in Nagayama Prefecture — were restored to their original states, igniting a national fascination with the Sengoku period that had been suppressed during the occupation years.

RTK, who had been struggling to attract viewers from RMT's ''Onnamono'' Dramas (both RTK and RMT would merge in 1964 to create the present KTK), changed its strategy towards television broadcasting and began to research on possible programs. Sugihara Shinichi, the president at that time, suggested to target the male demographic and young people, so a series of 10-minute shorts called ''Samurai Monogatari'' were created. Much to Sugihara's prediction, the response had been overwhelming positive, so he expanded the ''Samurai Monogatari'' to 30 minutes, eventually creating the Jidaigeki genre commonly found in Sanese Television.

As more books about the Okaiken samurai were being published, RTK began to address authors and manga artists to write up a Jidaigeki broadcast that would be the response to the ''Onnamono'' dramas every week. Sanese author Nishida Goromaru came up with an idea of an annual hour long broadcast of an important person in Sanese History every week. Several producers and directors supported the idea, and RTK adopted the Taisen drama concept in June 7, 1960.

The very first series of the Taisen drama, ''Hana no Shogai'', was met with mixed reviews and were unable to beat the high ratings of the ''Onnamono'' drama ''Oya no Mono''. RTK was ready to cancel the concept after the show ended in 1961 but Nishida insisted that the Taisen drama can become a great success by using written content of authors as well as new concepts being proposed by writers at that time, such as an emphasis over battle strategies, brutality and consequential actions. RTK hired Hirooka Matsushiro to create the second and third Taisen dramas. The third series that Matsushiro created through RTK, ''Taikō no Mondai'', achieved critical and commercial success, and thus the concept of the Taisen drama was finalized in August 14, 1963.

Throughout the late 1960s, the Taisen drama became a staple of Sanese Sunday nights. A major technological milestone was reached in 1969 with ''Ten to Chi to'', which was the first series broadcast in color, coinciding with the rise of color television sets across Okaiken. This era also saw the genre experiment with time periods beyond the Sengoku, such as ''Mukashi no Koto'' (1964), which explored the ancient Maki Period.

The 1980s are often cited as the "Golden Age" of the Taisen drama, characterized by record-breaking viewership. In 1983, ''Honda Shigetomo'' captivated the nation, achieving an unprecedented 41.6% average viewing audience. This era solidified many series conventions, including the "epic opening" and the use of high-profile cinematic actors. The 1980s also saw the first non-Sanese lead in 1981 with ''Captain Miura Anjin'', portraying the adventures of an Insulonian sailor.

In the 2000s, KTK began utilizing High Definition broadcasting, starting with ''Sengoku Risshiden'' in 2000. This period was marked by a shift toward more complex, character-driven narratives, such as the two-part epic ''Bishamonten'' and ''Fūrin Kazan'' in 2011/2012. The most significant modern milestone occurred in 2014 with ''Honda Tennō'', which utilized 4K resolution for the first time and surpassed all previous viewership records with a staggering 44.9% average.

In recent years, the genre has continued to push boundaries. Series 51, ''Bayan'' (2012), was the first to be set entirely outside of Okaiken, while the 60th series, ''Oni-Motonobu'' (2021), revitalized interest in the mid-Sengoku period by focusing on the visceral physicality of the "Demon Admiral." The 65th series, ''Hatsu: Shiokaze no Kōgō'' (2026–2027), marked the first time a Taisen drama centered on a woman since ''Onna Bugeisha'' in 1993, exploring the life of Hatsu-no-kata, daughter of Anjō Motonobu and primary wife of Honda Shigetada, whose maritime connections sustained the Honda clan through the unification wars.

As the series approaches its 65th anniversary, the Taisen drama remains the premier medium for exploring Sanese national identity through the lens of history. Filming locations have increasingly utilised the restored castles of the Sengoku period — Suemori Castle in Nagayama Prefecture, Matsushiro Castle in Kōchi, and the grounds of Okami Castle on Harujima — lend authenticity to the battle sequences that define the genre. The network has also invested in extensive location shooting across all three principal islands: the volcanic highlands of Ishijima for the ''Oni-Motonobu'' naval sequences, the Inland Sea coast of Yagikoshu for the ''Bishamonten'' cavalry charges, and the Kantō plain surrounding Okami for the ''Honda Tennō'' coronation scenes.

===2027–2028: ''Naotaka''===

On 9 July 2026, KTK announced that the 66th Taisen drama, ''Naotaka'' (直孝), would premiere in April 2027. The series will chronicle the life of [[Ii Naotaka]], son of the legendary "Red Devil" general [[Ii Naomasa]] and childhood friend of [[Honda Shigetomo]]. The drama explores Naotaka's tragic arc — from his upbringing as a hostage alongside Shigetomo at Otomo Castle, through his father's rift with Shigetada over the leniency dispute following the [[Battle of Tone]], to the final confrontation at Ichijoudani in 1625 where Naotaka killed both his father and his former friend. Described by producers as "the mirror image of the 1983 ''Honda Shigetomo'' series," ''Naotaka'' will retell the Sengoku period from the perspective of the man history remembers as the betrayer, examining the personal cost of loyalty in a shattered nation. The role of Naotaka will be played by [[Ryunosuke Kamiki]], with [[Takayama Kenta]] reprising his role as Anjō Motonobu from ''Oni-Motonobu'' (2021) and [[Fujioka Hiroshi]] reprising Honda Shigetada from ''Honda Tennō'' (2014).
