|}


==Controversies==

The Taisen drama has not been without its critics. Conservative scholars have periodically accused the series of prioritising spectacle over historical accuracy, particularly in its depiction of battle scenes where the scale of armies and the choreography of combat are often exaggerated for dramatic effect. The network has generally defended these choices as necessary concessions to the visual medium, though it has occasionally issued corrections when public outcry over specific inaccuracies has been significant.

===The Akō Rōshi beheading incident (1962)===

The foundational controversy of the Taisen drama occurred during its second series, ''Akō Rōshi'' (1962), when the climactic beheading of Lord Kira was broadcast in a prolonged close-up that drew hundreds of viewer complaints and prompted a Diet inquiry. The scene — in which Ōishi Kuranosuke holds the severed head aloft by the topknot — was deemed excessively graphic for a Sunday evening broadcast and led to the establishment of KTK's first Content Standards Guidelines in 1963. The guidelines codified rules for the depiction of violence, establishing a framework that persists in modified form to the present day. Every subsequent content dispute involving the Taisen drama — from the disembowelment scenes in ''Kusa Moeru'' (1979) to the mass casualty sequences in ''Oni-Motonobu'' (2021) — has been adjudicated against the precedent set by the Akō Rōshi incident. The controversy is now regarded as a defining moment in the history of Sanese broadcasting, establishing the principle that the Taisen drama's commitment to martial realism must be balanced against the public's right to be informed without being subjected to gratuitous violence.

===Hatsu controversy (2026–present)===

The 65th series, ''Hatsu: Shiokaze no Kōgō'' (2026–2027), has generated the most significant public controversy since the Akō Rōshi incident, drawing coverage from both ''[[The Okami Times]]'' and the ''[[Toyozawa Mainichi]]'' and escalating into a Diet-level review of KTK's editorial independence.

====The Sai River scene====

On 10 June 2026, Dr. Masahide Takeda, the historical consultant for the series, held a press conference at the Toyozawa Press Club accusing KTK of censoring a four-minute scene depicting the deliberate burning of civilian shelters during the [[Siege of Sai River]] in Nagato Prefecture. The scene — which had been filmed and was in the final cut two weeks prior — was the emotional pivot of the series, depicting the moment Hatsu-no-kata turns against her own commanders after witnessing the atrocity. Without it, the character's transformation relies on a battlefield conversation with no historical basis. Dr. Takeda stated: ''"They'll show a horse gutted by a pike but not families burning. The issue is not graphic content. The issue is that the civilian victims were inconvenient for the narrative KTK wanted to tell."'' Director Yui Kondo confirmed: ''"I filmed it. I fought for it. The cut was not my decision."'' KTK responded: ''"All editorial decisions reflect broadcasting standards."'' The history department of Toyozawa University issued a statement supporting Dr. Takeda.

====#TellTheTruth campaign====

The hashtag #TellTheTruth trended on PYRAMID30 following Dr. Takeda's press conference, reaching 3.4 million cumulative mentions by 17 June and 4.1 million by the time the finale aired. A petition demanding the release of the director's cut of the finale reached 1.6 million signatures. KTK did not respond to the petition. Director Kondo Yui did not speak publicly after the initial statement.

====Diet Communications Committee hearings====

The Diet Communications Committee opened hearings into the controversy on 12 June 2026, summoning KHR Director-General Matsumoto and the KHR editorial committee. Testimony revealed that the KTK Programming Department was informed of the Hatsu finale running time change on 2 June — four days before the decision to cut the civilian shelter scene was reported publicly. The Broadcasting Council, which includes government observers, was not informed until 5 June. The testimony exposed a structural blind spot in the pillarized broadcasting system: the coordination body manages schedule, not content, meaning a decision to alter a historically significant scene was made within a single pillar's editorial apparatus, invisible to the cross-pillar coordination structure. KHK (the Kwangju Pillar representative) addressed the chamber: ''"The right to tell the truth, even when the truth is about the Imperial Army."'' Nakayama proposed a both-versions solution: air the cut scene as a special feature alongside the edited finale.

====Finale broadcast====

The two-hour Hatsu finale aired on 14 June 2026 at 8 PM — opposite the AFA World Cup opening ceremony in Balisca, a scheduling clash that generated its own debate under the hashtag #HatsuOrWorldCup. The finale aired '''without''' the cut scene. The series drew a 35.4% average rating for its Siege of Sai River arc (Episode 11), the highest-rated Taisen episode since ''Oni-Motonobu'' in 2021, with Kasumi Arimura's seven-minute continuous take drawing particular acclaim. The ''Toyozawa Mainichi'' editorialised that the finale's impact lay in what was absent: ''"The two stories — national pride in football and national memory of civilian suffering — both matter. But the Hatsu finale's impact lies in the cut scenes."'' The Communications Committee's 14-day reporting deadline expired after the finale aired. As of July 2026, the committee hearings continue, and KTK has not announced whether the cut scene will be restored.

====Connection to the Akō Rōshi precedent====

The Hatsu controversy has been widely compared to the 1962 Akō Rōshi beheading incident, with both cases involving the tension between the Taisen drama's commitment to historical realism and the broadcaster's content standards. However, commentators have noted a critical difference: the Akō Rōshi controversy was about whether to show violence; the Hatsu controversy is about whether to show the '''consequences''' of violence — specifically, civilian suffering. As the ''Okami Times'' editorialised: ''"The Akō Rōshi precedent established that the broadcaster has a right to regulate graphic content. The Hatsu case asks whether that right extends to suppressing historically significant content because it is politically inconvenient."'' The outcome of the Communications Committee review is expected to redefine the Content Standards Guidelines for the first time since their establishment in 1963.

===Other controversies===

* '''Casting of Kasumi Arimura''': The decision to cast a 28-year-old actress as a woman who, historically, was between 15 and 40 during the events depicted, drew criticism from both sides — some arguing the actress is too young for the later scenes, others arguing she is too old for the early ones.
* '''The Motonobu question''': The series depicts Anjō Motonobu as a complex, sympathetic figure rather than the "Demon Admiral" of popular imagination. This has been controversial among viewers who grew up with the 2021 series ''Oni-Motonobu'', which portrayed him as a near-mythical warrior. Takayama Kenta, who reprises the role, has defended the portrayal as showing "the man behind the demon."
* '''The Naotaka prequel controversy''': The announcement of the 66th series, ''Naotaka'' (2027–2028), which will retell the same historical period from Ii Naotaka's perspective, has led to accusations that KTK is "milking" the Sengoku period and that the two series will contradict each other on key events. Producers have stated that the differing perspectives are intentional and reflect the inherent ambiguity of historical memory.


